    cross-device sync unless you later add a server.
  - [1mNode CLI[0m — preferences live in a JSON file ([38;5;153m~/.config/<app>/preferences.json[39m or similar). Buys you real
    cross-invocation persistence for the CLI and something testable without a DOM. Costs: irrelevant to the login page;
    needs path resolution and file-error handling.
  - [1mBoth, over a shared module[0m — one preferences API in [38;5;153msrc/[39m, with a swappable storage backend (localStorage adapter /
    file adapter). Buys you one schema and one set of tests. Costs: the repo has no bundler, so sharing [38;5;153msrc/[39m code with
    [38;5;153mapp.js[39m means either adding one or duplicating — real work for a benefit nothing currently needs.

  My recommendation is [1mbrowser / localStorage[0m, on the evidence that the only user-facing surface here is the login page
  — [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is a hello-world with no user to have preferences. Going browser-only now is cheap to reverse: if the
  CLI later needs settings, the schema and defaults lift out into a shared module, and only the read/write calls change.
  Going "both" now is the expensive, hard-to-undo choice, since it drags in a build step.

